Hello,

I have reports that KDE 2.1 (or 2.1.1) was running very slow on older DEC
hardware. So I sat down this morning and made a test on our old DEC
3000/600, 21064 175MHz, 128MB RAM with an 8-bit Pseudocolor display,
running Tru64 Unix Version 5.0, PK2.

I've used KDE 2.1.1 and a fresh user account where KDE was never running
before. Here are my timings:

- login to KDE takes: about 1 minute.
- opening a konsole terminal window: 2-5 seconds
- opening kmail: 10-15 seconds
- opening kcontrol (control center): 5-10 seconds
- opening konqueror (web browser): 15-25 seconds
- moving windows on the display (even with their contents) is just as fast
  as on modern machines.

I think that these numbers are quite acceptable, given that this hardware
is so old. Also the machine was not running idle. Appended is a process
list of the machine where one KDE 2.1.1 session is running.

Note that if you are using a 16 or 24-bit Truecolor visual on the same
configuration, KDE will likely run much slower because much more RAM is
required to save the bitmaps. Running on an 8-bit PseudoColor visual
showed no problems at all! All KDE apps were running smoothly but with
dithered colors, of course. The only problem are legacy applications which
require lots of colors from the 8-bit PseudoColor visual which are not
available anymore when KDE2 is running. There is a special black&white
color theme available in kcontrol->look&feel->themes which could be used
to remedy this situation. I have not tried this yet, and I'd appreciate
getting any reports of people who run KDE2 on 8-bit PseudoColor visuals
with legacy apps.

Now if you get considerable worse timings on your older (or even newer DEC
hardware) there must be something wrong in your configuration. These
are the most common configuration problems:

a.) Network, especially DNS setup problems. When DNS is not configured
    properly, DNS lookups get a timeout and slow down everything.

b.) Invalid LD_LIBRARY_PATH settings. If you've got your LD_LIBRARY_PATH
    pointing to an invalid location, this might also slow things down
    considerably. To check this, please log-in to the user account via
    telnet and check the LD_LIBRARY_PATH variable.

Tom

