Coming from Linux background is better than coming from no background, but 
there still are many differences between Linux and T64.

First, the place to look for information:  
In Linux, your info is scattered about hundreds of sites, and you just have 
to know which ones, including freshmeat, to look at.
In T64, you go to http://tru64unix.compaq.com/ for pretty much all your 
needs.  Specifically, see 
   Freeware http://tru64unix.compaq.com/demos/
   Patches  http://www.support.compaq.com/patches/

   Documentation  (Be SURE TO USE LIBRARY.HTM !!!!!)
 http://www.tru64unix.compaq.com/faqs/publications/base_doc/DOCUMENTATION/

Most software that comes as a tarball of source code will work on T64, 
however, if it was written for Linux, chances are you're missing some 
necessary libraries.

I've heard (but not tested) that the Linux libraries come with T64 v5.0 and 
up, but with v4.0, you're gonna have to either forget things written for 
Linux specifically, or figure out what dependencies you've not met.

To permanently add swap space -- No, simply adding the fstab entry isn't 
enough.  I'm sure this is one of the things covered in that Documentation 
secion though, so I suggest starting there.

