The dump and restore respective vdump and vrestore programs can do 100% 
logically identical copies of filesystems. I.e. mount your new disk, 
create partitions / advfs domains / ... on it, mount them then use 
something like

dump <arguments> | restore <arguments>

to copy the data over to the new disk. 

The detailed procedure is a little bit tricky, e.g. if you use advfs 
you must use vdump instead of dump and create a new domain on the new 
disk, and modify fstab and /etc/fdmns after copying to adapt to the new 
domain name. Also, don't forget about the the swap partition

In case you need a step-by-step instruction, you'd have to provide us 
with much more detailed info. Like Tru64 Unix version numer you use, 
file systems you use, output of the "mount" command.

It definitely works this way if done right. :-)

