What version of the operating system?  What file systems?

On V4 and earlier, you can use dump or vdump to copy each file
system.  Use dump for UFS and vdump for AdvFS.  For AdvFS you'll
need to include the option to write the AdvFS boot block when
you label the disk.  I believe the default is to use the UFS
boot block.  For non-root file systems using AdvFS, you can
migrate a file system from one disk to another by adding the
target partition with the addvol command then removing the
original partition with rmvol.  You need the AdvFS Utilities
license to do this.

The presense of LSM may complicate this since you also have to
deal with the extra layer.

On V5 it gets even more complicated because the system disk
remembers what device it is on.  My recollection is that when
customers first started installing V5 and moving system disks
around the old way, they had problems.  If you can find the
Best Practices document it may describe how to do this sort
of transition on V5.  You might also check the system management
documentation for disaster recovery procedures and treat your
situation as "restoring a system backup to a new disk".

The information you want is really version dependent and you
didn't offer a clue what that was.

