In article <3ABB751D.31EE998D@abdn.ac.uk>, Igor Kozin <i.kozin@abdn.ac.uk> writes:

>How to increase the memory limit a user can use?
>I have not set any but apparently the system does not
>allow to allocate more than 128MB.

Virtual memory limits get imposed in a number of different places.

The most relevant for you is the per-process limits.  These limits
come in two values; the default and the maximum.  There are limits
for data space (128 MB default and 1 GB maximum) and stack space
(2 or 8 MB and 32 MB).  The particular command depends on the
shell, though the usual choices are "limit" and "ulimit".  See
you shell's manual page for particulars.

The default and maximum values of the various limits are system
configuration parameters and will be documented in the sys_attrs_proc(5)
manual page (if present on your version).  The sysconfig(8) and
dxkerneltuner(8) commands can be used to change these limits if you
want to raise the default or maximum.

After these, page and swap space is usually the limiting factor
of virtual memory use, though the clever programmer may discover
how to use the mmap(2) system to get around a short of page and
swap space.  Older versions have a maximum address space limit,
though this may have been removed in V5.

