	tcpwrappers and ssh2 go together quite nicely.  Of course you'd have to
disable any non-essential services on the system; don't forget about
x-windows.
	Of the services you do think you need, assess the threat and see if you
can reduce the risk.
	CERT is definately the place to get good advice from.  There's a
document there showing how to build ssh with tcpwrappers.  Those same
instructions are also in a README in the ssh2 source.
	You can configure tcpwrappers to notify you via email when you get a
connect attempt from someplace you shouldn't.  This is a really nice
feature to alert you immediately.  You can also send stuff back to the
client, such as a nasty message telling them they're bad.  Careful about
this though because it can make you vulnerable to a denial of service
(DOS) attack.  Look for the twist directive in the tcpwrappers
documentation.
	IMHO, one of the biggest threats to university infrastructures is
cleartext passwords because the network is open to sniffing moreso than
anywhere else and the level of expertise is concentrated.

	Cheers,
		Mario

