In article <3AB63FEF.D4896273@hotmail.com>, Mario Stargard <mstargard@hotmail.com> writes:
|> 	ipcs -a tells you how many processes are attached (NATTCH) to the
|> shared memory segment, who the creating pid (CPID) was and the last pid
|> (LPID) to attach to the segment.  This doesn't tell you the pids of the
|> currently attached processes, which is what I'm looking for.
|> 	Thanks, though.
|> 
|> 	Mario
|> 


Just to make it clear what Mario and I are after, /proc/pid/map under 
Solaris returns an array of

typedef struct prmap {
          uintptr_t pr_vaddr;        /* virtual address of mapping */
          size_t pr_size;            /* size of mapping in bytes */
          char pr_mapname[PRMAPSZ];  /* name in /proc/pid/object */
          offset_t pr_offset;        /* offset into mapped object, if any */
          int pr_mflags;             /* protection and attribute flags */
          int pr_pagesize;           /* pagesize for this mapping in bytes */
          int pr_shmid;              /* SysV shared memory identifier */
         } prmap_t;

which conveniently gives the required info as the last component. 

The only clue I have found is that the address in the corresponding struct
under Tru64 is very small - not something to rely on :^)


