If you physically have the disk, then you're physically capable of 
installing the OS.  However, most services are not functional until you 
load the license information into the machine.  For example, until you load 
the license information, you can only login on the local console with one 
user at a time.  But there are tons of other restrictions.

There are lots of licensing options -- Compaq wants you to use Tru64, so 
they make a lot of options available to you.  I don't know how much it 
costs, but I can say this:
If all of the functionality you need can be run by Tru64, my experience is 
that Tru64 is more reliable than Linux.  If you want some service not 
supported by Tru64 (for example, IP Masquerading) then -- it's possible to 
make it work, but if you're building a crappy little router anyway, why 
would you pay for Tru64?
I digress.
The point is, there are a few differences between them.  It is wise to 
evaluate your options, pros/cons ahead of time.  But as you say you want a 
machine to handle users and light workload, ... it's hard to say what's 
best, both are very good.
I suggest you post a question, Tru64 vs Linux, ask in this group and also 
in comp.os.linux.alpha.  You'll get a lot of people offering good advice.  
Just give them some good information when you ask.


