In article <97vtb8$sfp$1@twin1.cbr.ru>, Victor Lipin <lvv@cbr.ru> wrote:
> Is it a good idea?
> 
> - would 233 MHz and 64 MB RAM be enough to run Tru64 and some lightweight
> tasks?

Forgetting about the licensing issues others have mentioned, it's my opinion
that 64 MB is a bare minimum for running Tru64 or any other truly 64 bit
OS.  You can certainly run whatever programs you want as long as you
configure enough swap, but it's going to be a poor performer, becausing you're
going to be spending a lot of time paging and even swapping. 

You'll need to disable as much as possible to free up as much RAM as you
can for the stuff you want to run.  Buying more RAM would be better.

Just remember that each and every pointer on a truly 64 bit OS is 8 bytes,
as are longs.  That essentially means that programs often require a little
or sometimes a lot more RAM on a 64 bit OS.

> - is it possible to upgrade memory with ordinary PC FPM SIMMs?

We have both a 1000 and 1000a, and I seem to recall that they take "standard"
SIMMs, but I have a vague recollection of them needing to be installed in
banks of 5 -- 4 simms + 1 used for parity.  It's been a *long* time since I've
cracked open either of those systems, though, so I could be totally
misremembering things.

> - is it possible to use ordinary SCSI-2 HDD disks and CDD-ROM?

Yes, but be advised that if you want to use both the internal StorageWorks
shelf (which requires drives to be in an SBB cannister) and externally
connected devices, you'll probably need a second SCSI controller.  There is
an external 50-pin connector on the back of the 1000 and 1000a, but you can't
run both an on-board and external SCSI bus off the one SCSI controller.

> - if I download Tru64 v5.0a 3 ISO images from Internet, should I have any
> licence keys for installation?

Yes.

> I'm planning to use this product only for
> non-commercial purposes! How to obtain Tru64?
> - how long this retired hardware will be supported in next Tru64 releases? I
> can't find any info about hardware supported by Tru64.

If you're truly not going to use this for commercial purposes *and* you
don't plan on having a number of distinct users logged in at once, you
should buy the $99.00 copy of Tru64 for "Hobbyists", and then you're legal
and you get the (much better) 5.1 version on CD anyway.

Unlike other traditional UNIX vendor OSes (e.g. Solaris) available for
cheap, the Tru64 hobbyist license comes with very good compiler/developer
support.

Start at

	http://www.tru64unix.compaq.com

and try the `Buy' followed by `Noncommercial' links.

> I don't have any expirience with Tru64 and any DEC hardware, but I have
> intermediate to excellent knowledge of HP-UX and HP9000, Solaris, SCO,
> Linux. Is it possible to learn Tru64 and AlphaServer hardware basics only
> with free available documentation?

It's possible.  Note that recent versions of HP-UX (i.e. about 10.20 and
later) are quite similar to Tru64 from startup (i.e. run-level scripts)
and configuration.  There are certainly lots and lots of differences, but
HP-UX comes closest to the FS layout of Tru64.  I'm always looking in
the wrong spot for startup scripts (/etc/init.d, or Linux's goofy
/etc/rc.d/init.d) on Solaris, IRIX, and Linux -- I'm used to /sbin/init.d
from HP-UX and Tru64.

Tim
