> 2.) I could no longer mount network drives onto PC's on the
>     LAN via Windows Explorer from the AS4100's. Once again
>     it kept coming up with an "Invalid Password" error.
>
> I believe the two problems are linked sue to the same error
> happening in both. Now that I have gone back to Base Security
> the problems have gone away. I know that my password was correct
> as I was able to login using that same password.

First, Enhanced Security "can" use a different algorithm for determining
the password - it also respects characters 9 through 80 when typed.
This is on a Base Security system the passwords "password1" and
"password" result in the same string written to /etc/passwd because only
the 1st 8 characters are taken.  On an ES system, those passwords are
unique...

Second, I don't know about the packages you were using, but many
packages will authenticate users by calling "getpwnam" followed by a
"crypt" of some passed password and finally an "strncmp" of the result
of the crypt with the pw_passwd field.  On ES that won't work because a
"*" is replaced in /etc/passwd for the real password which is store in
auth.db (/var/tcb/files/auth.db or /tcb/files/auth.db).

--

John Ferlan
Tru64 UNIX Security
