>>Probably about 0% of the systems are in clusters but a lot more than 0%
>>of the revenue comes from clusters.
>
>Eh? This comment is over my head. Care to elaborate?

I think a large majority of Tru64 systems are not using clusters.

Those that do use clusters produce a disproportionate share of
revenue because they are BIG installations, running expensive
base hardware with even more expensive cluster hardware.
The cluster software isn't cheap either.

In the early 1990s computer makers decided to go beyond 32 bit
addressing.  They didn't expect to sell many systems with more
than 4 GB memory over the next few years (even today the market
share of >4GB systems is small), but every time they sold one
they made hundreds of thousands of dollars profit on the RAM alone.

-- 
    John Carr (jfc@mit.edu)


