In article <3aa400de.421702896@news.telusplanet.net>, trevor.osatchuk@nospam.pscl.com (Trevor Osatchuk) writes:

>Pretty simple question to which I have not been able to find a simple
>answer.  Is there a way to monitor total virtual memory and total
>actual memory used by a single process?  Top will give a SIZE and RES
>value in kilobytes, but I have heard that it may not be the most
>accurate program around.  I want to know if there is another tool that
>can give me process by process vm usage and physical memory usage.

To verify the general quality of top's data, compare the VM and
RSS sizes for given process as listed by top and by ps(1).  If
they're the same, then that's about the as good as you're going
to get without going deep within the kernel to get the data.
Both top and ps(1) probably use the various I/O controls that
are available with the process file system; proc(4).  You can
change how the data is reported by writing your own program, but
the basic data won't change.

One of the things that complicates matter is how you want to
count shared pages.  Should a process count shared page as
