In article <3aa44e9d@news.starhub.net.sg>, system <wongpk@tuteee.starhub.net.sg> writes:

>Hi there is there a way I can see what RAID my system is running running?

Probably.  Without much clue as to the what sort of system you have,
what operating system it is running, what version of that operating
system and what the I/O configuration is, it will be hard to provide
detailed advice.  I'll assume (unsafely of course), that since you
posted in comp.unix.tru64 that you have an Alpha system running
Tru64 UNIX, Digital UNIX or DEC OSF/1.

The two most widely used and supported RAID subsystems available on
Tru64 UNIX are:

o  A family of backplane array controllers originally from Mylex.
   On V4 and earlier versions these show up as "re" devices.  I
   the system boot messages identifies the RAID level of each
   logical unit when the device is initially discovered.  These
   boot messages are kept in /var/adm/messages and the binary
   error log.  DECevent and uerf(8) will format the error log.
   See the manual pages for those commands for your information.

o  The HS family of StorageWorks array controllers.  Over the years
   there have been CI connected controllers (HSJ), DSSI (HSD), SCSI
   (HSZ) and Fibre Channel (HSG).  The HSZ and HSG versions of the
   controllers are those most likely to appear on Tru64 UNIX systems.
   HSJ family controllers may work, but aren't supported.  No DSSI
   adapters are supported by Tru64 UNIX, so the HSD controllers aren't
   relevant.

In addition to these supported hardware subsystems, you may have
3rd party subsystems that we never qualified.  You'll have to
consult that vendor's documentation to determine what RAID level
a particular device is using.

Finally, Tru64 UNIX supports a variety of software RAID levels
via the Logical Storage Manager and the Advanced File System.
Some "devices" and file systems on your system may use these.

Getting the basic RAID configuration of the backplane array controllers
should be pretty easy.  These subsystems also have a standalone
configuration utility that you can use to get more detailed
information than just the RAID level of a logical unit.

With the HS family it is a bit more complicated.  These appear to
the host as ordinary units of the underlying storage protocol;
SCSI or MSCP.  Neither protocol has much clue how an underlying
unit is constructed, so there's nothing in the boot messages to
offer a clue what the RAID level is.  Most of the family does
support connecting a terminal to the controller console port
so that you can do configuration.  The SHOW command will give
you information about the controllers, device, storage sets
and logical units.  Check the subsystem documentation to find
out the details of the various SHOW options.

On systems which have hszterm and hsxterm available, it may be
possible to use these commands to connect to the controller's
virtual console and do the various SHOW commands.

The Solutions kits of the newer versions of these subsystem include
the StorageWorks Command Console (SWCC).  If this is installed and
setup, you can use it get configuration information.  I believe
the typical SWCC setup is an agent on the system where the sub-
system is connected and a GUI on a Windows NT system.

On the software side of things, AdvFS supports striping on a per-
file basis.  Check the manual page for the stripe(8) command to
see what other AdvFS command is used to see if a file is striped.
AdvFS striping requires the AdvFS utilities license.

LSM in V4 and earlier supports striping, mirroring, those two
combined (mirrored stripe sets) and volume concatenation.  The
disk with the root file system on it can be mirrored.  The version
of LSM available on V5 adds RAID-5 to the suite.  The volprint(8)
command should be a good starting place to see how a volume is
setup.

